Description: Default the dump folder to the user's own space where the executable is not writable
 Upstream defaults the output folder to a "Dumps" subdirectory beside the
 executable. That fits a portable build, unpacked by the user into a directory
 the user owns. A distribution package instead installs the executable into a
 root-owned system directory -- /usr/lib64/redumper-gui here -- where creating
 that folder fails with EACCES. src/dump.rs discards the error from
 create_dir_all(), so the dump does not stop with "permission denied": it runs
 on and fails later, for a reason that never names the cause.
 .
 Probe whether the executable's own directory is writable, and fall back to a
 Dumps folder in the user's home where it is not. The macOS branch of
 default_output_dir() already does exactly this -- there the executable lives
 inside a read-only .app bundle -- and it is left untouched, keeping the download
 folder its users expect. Portable builds are unaffected on either platform:
 their directory is writable, so the probe passes and nothing moves.
